// THE SECRET OF SECRETS IN PLAIN WORDS: a close paraphrase of each passage of
// Ismail Ali's English of the Arabic (Steele 1920, pp. 176–266), written for
// this archive. The translator's square brackets are his explanations and are
// followed here; letters in round brackets such as "(B)" or "C only" are
// Steele's notes of which manuscript has a reading, printed inside the text,
// and the paraphrase mentions them only where they matter. Where the English
// is uncertain or the sense turns on a doubtful word, the paraphrase ends with
// "Other possible readings".

export const PLAIN_SECRETUM: Record<string, string> = {
  "arab.secretum.0.1":
    "The book opens with a blessing on the Commander of the Faithful (the caliph). His servant says he has obeyed the order to study the book on governing a state called the Secret of Secrets, which the philosopher Aristotle wrote for his pupil King Alexander, son of Philip of Macedon, known as Dhu 'l-Karnain ('the two-horned'), when Aristotle was too old and ill to follow him on campaign.\n\nAlexander had made Aristotle his chief minister and close friend; Aristotle was wise, learned, pious and just, so that some count him among the prophets. Some say he died a natural death and has a known tomb; others that he was taken up to heaven in a column of light. Alexander conquered by following his advice. In one of their letters, Alexander wrote from Persia that he meant to kill its able and ambitious men; Aristotle answered that he could not kill their land and climate, and should win them by kindness instead. Alexander did, and the Persians became his most loyal subjects.\n\nThen the translator, Yuhanna ibn al-Batrik, tells how he searched every temple where philosophers had hidden their writings until, at the temple of the Sun built by Askalabiyus (Asclepius), a learned priest let him see them. He found the book there and translated it from Greek into Syriac and from Syriac into Arabic. It begins with Aristotle's letter to Alexander.",
  "arab.secretum.0.2":
    "Aristotle answers Alexander's letter. He did not stay behind for lack of love but because of age and illness. What Alexander asks for is too great even for living hearts, let alone paper; he will write it down, but Alexander must not press him to reveal more than this book holds. He has hidden some forbidden mysteries in dark hints, so that the book will not help wicked men if it falls into their hands, and he binds Alexander to keep its secrets as he himself was bound.\n\nThe first rule: a king needs two helpers. One is inward, the strength and unity of his own soul, as a kingdom is strong when its subjects are united. The harmony between ruler and ruled is won in two ways, one open and one secret. The open way is generosity and justice; the secret way belongs to the saints and sages whom God has taught, and Aristotle will reveal it in certain chapters. The book is outwardly a treasury of wisdom and rules, and inwardly the hidden prize itself.\n\nThen the ten discourses are listed: I, the kinds of kings; II, the king's character and conduct; III, justice; IV, ministers; V, scribes; VI, ambassadors; VII, governors and tax collectors; VIII, generals and officers; IX, war, its tactics, its timing and the name of the commander; X, the magical sciences, astrology, winning people over, and the virtues of minerals and herbs.",
  "arab.secretum.1.1":
    "Discourse I, on the kinds of kings. The text announces four kinds but lists three: a king generous to himself and to his people; generous to himself and mean to his people; mean to himself and mean to his people. The Rumi (Byzantines) see no harm in a king mean to himself and generous to his people; the Indians think he should be mean to both; the Persians, generous to both. All agree that generosity to himself with meanness to his people ruins a kingdom.\n\nEvery quality goes bad at its extremes. True generosity is giving what is needed, when it is needed, to those who deserve it, as far as one can. Giving what is not needed, or at the wrong time, or to the undeserving is waste, like giving water to a man standing on a river bank, or helping an enemy against oneself. Meanness is unworthy of a king; if he is mean by nature, he should leave his gifts to a trusted friend. A king who gives more than his state can bear destroys himself and it; real generosity, as Hermes the Great wrote, is keeping one's hands off the people's property. The kings of Hanaj fell because they took too much from their people, who prayed at the Temple of Lightning until God sent a wind that destroyed them. Wealth is part of life itself.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The fourth kind of king (presumably one mean to himself and generous to his people, the kind the Rumi approve) is missing from the list as printed.",
  "arab.secretum.1.2":
    "Part of true generosity is not to look for excuses to harm others, not to pry into their hidden faults, and not to talk about one's own gifts. The height of virtue is to stop finding fault, to honour the generous, to meet people with a cheerful face and return their greetings, and to overlook the faults of the ignorant.\n\nThen a short rule that would be enough on its own: reason is the head of government and judgement, the health of the soul and the mirror of faults, and the source of everything praiseworthy. Its first tool is the wish for a good name. A king who rules for the sake of a good name is right; one who loves power for its own sake falls into tyranny. Tyranny breaks the bond with the people; that brings enmity, enmity brings division, division hatred, hatred war, and war destroys law and civilisation and goes against nature itself. Seeking glory and a good name leads the other way: to sincerity, piety, courtesy, friendship, generosity, and so to law and civilisation, and leaves a lasting name.\n\nAlexander must not feed his desires. Lust strengthens the animal soul, stains the body and troubles the spirit; it leads step by step to love of luxury, avarice, love of money, meanness, greed, treachery, robbery, loss of honour and strife, which destroy faith and love and ruin the world.",
  "arab.secretum.2.1":
    "Discourse II, on the king's character and the conduct proper to him. A king should be known by a special name and title, since everyone looks to him. A king who puts his state under the Law deserves his rank; one who takes his rank lightly is destroyed by it. He must keep every divine command, be a public example of temperance and true faith (hypocrisy never succeeds, for the people always see), overlook his subjects' small failings even at a cost, and honour judges, teachers and religious leaders. He should be ambitious without arrogance, open-minded, sharp and far-sighted, merciful, and never act in the first heat of anger; even righteous anger should be acted on without violence.\n\nHe should dress splendidly and have a fine, clear, strong voice, but raise it rarely, so that people do not grow used to him and despise him. He should speak mostly in reply and seldom mix with the common people. The Indians do this well: their king shows himself only from a distance, among guards, except once a year, when a minister praises God and the people's loyalty, the king hears petitions, gives justice and gifts and pardons; people remember the day and teach their children to love him, so plots fail.\n\nHe should lower taxes, above all on visiting merchants, so that trade and revenue grow. He should seek lasting things, above all a good name, and not live like beasts that grab and follow their appetites. Lechery is the habit of swine; it wastes body and life and makes a man soft.",
  "arab.secretum.2.2":
    "A king may take his pleasures with his household for three or four days at a time, while his court is told he is busy with important business, and he should keep trusted informants. Two or three times a year he may feast with his favourite courtiers, praising them, giving them wine and robes of honour, and rewarding loyalty with greater gifts. He should be grave and laugh little, since much laughter destroys dignity and brings on old age. Disrespect at court is punished: a great man by exclusion until he learns manners, deliberate contempt by death. Indian books say a king rules or is ruled according to the strength of his mind; Aesculapius says the best ruler is a vulture among carcasses, not a carcass among vultures.\n\nLoyalty comes from religion, love, hope and fear. Remove the people's grievances and do not let them speak out, for those who can speak can act; fear of the king is the peace of the realm. The king is like rain or wind, which God sends as blessings though they sometimes destroy; people still thank God for them. A king is the same.\n\nAlexander should look after the weak and help them from the treasury in famine, through an honest official; reassure good people and make troublemakers feel watched; and store grain against famine, opening all the stores when it comes.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- '[Persians B]' is Steele's note that one manuscript names the Persians instead of the Indians as the source of the saying about fear and the sword.",
  "arab.secretum.2.3":
    "Aristotle's strongest command: do not shed blood without cause or use the death penalty lightly, since a judge sees only appearances. Hermes the Great said that when one creature wrongly kills another the angels complain to God that his servant has tried to make himself like Him. If the dead man was a murderer, God answers that he killed and was killed; if he was killed for gain or on false suspicion, God declares the killer's blood forfeit, and the angels curse him. If he dies naturally after all, God's anger is greater still, and he is punished for ever unless he repents.\n\nWhere punishment is needed, long imprisonment or a hard flogging is usually enough; follow the writings of the divine ancestors. Treat the weakest enemy as if he were the strongest, since small enemies grow.\n\nTreachery is the nature of foolish children; it gains a little and loses much. Never break promises or covenants. Two spirits at Alexander's right and left record every word and deed for God. Nobody can force a king to swear, since only God's hand is above his; so he should swear only an oath he would keep under the knife. Inakh, Safoor and Hananij lost their kingdoms by breaking oaths for worldly gain. There are cases in statecraft where agreements must be broken, which the book will touch on later.",
  "arab.secretum.2.4":
    "Do not grieve for what is past; that is for women and the weak. Make the people study the arts and sciences and reward the learned; read petitions and reward good deeds. The Greeks became great through love of knowledge, so that even their daughters at home knew their religion and laws, the planets, the courses of the moon, astrology and medicine.\n\nDo not trust women's service, except a woman who values loyalty above her own life. Beware of poison; many kings have died of it. Do not trust one doctor, who may be bribed: have ten if possible, take only medicine they all agree on, and have it made in front of them and a trusted servant. Remember the Indian king's mother who sent a girl raised on poison until she was like a venomous snake; Aristotle saw through it and saved Alexander's life.\n\nGuard the immortal soul, and do not be like ignorant temple people who follow the letter without the meaning. If possible do nothing except at a time chosen by astrology. Through it Plato understood colours and invented painting on silk. Astrology is not false: no one escapes fate, but knowing it in advance lets people prepare, as for winter or famine, and pray to God to avert it.\n\nThe science of the stars has a part called astronomy (the spheres, planets and their motions) and the science of decrees (how the heavens foretell events below the moon); its roots are the planets, spheres and zodiac. The observed stars number 1,029.",
  "arab.secretum.2.5":
    "Aristotle will now give a secret of medicine that will free Alexander from needing doctors to keep healthy. Nothing in this world or the next is won without strength, strength depends on health, and health on the balance of the four humours. God taught the means through prophets, and the sages of India, Rome, Persia and Greece found more; the Greek school is the most reliable, and the book follows it.\n\nMan is made of opposing humours and needs food and drink: too much or too little brings disease, moderation brings health. Excess in eating or fasting, sleep or waking, exercise or rest, purging, bleeding or sex invites illness. Socrates ate very little; when a pupil urged him to eat more, he said he ate to live, not lived to eat. The best medicine is moderation in everything.\n\nHealth is kept in two ways: eating what suits one's constitution, the season and one's habits, and clearing out the excess the body produces. A warm, soft body digests hard food; a hard, dry one needs moist, mild food. Food should match one's temperament, and excess heat is treated with cold things. A strong warm stomach is like a big fire that can take logs; a weak cold one is like a small fire fed with twigs.\n\nGood health shows in a light body, regular bowels and good appetite; bad health in sluggishness, a puffy face, heavy eyes, wind, sour belching and loss of appetite, which if neglected lead to worse.",
  "arab.secretum.2.6":
    "The superior degree of counsel: Alexander's day. On waking he should walk a little, stretch and comb his hair; stretching firms the body and walking clears the vapours of sleep. Then wash, in summer in cold water; dress in clean, fine clothes, since the eye enjoys them and the spirit is strengthened. Clean the teeth with a stick of bitter, astringent wood: it cleans the mouth, loosens phlegm and stirs appetite. Take medicinal snuff suited to the season: it opens the brain, strengthens the senses and delays grey hair. Use perfume suited to the season, since scent feeds the soul and through it the body and heart. Chew a clove or nutmeg against phlegm.\n\nThen receive the great men, talk with them and do business,. When it is time to eat, first take exercise (wrestling, brisk walking, riding, lifting weights) to break up wind and kindle the stomach's heat. Then eat what he fancies, but not to fullness; take the loosening food before the binding food, and put slow-digesting food first so that it lies in the warmer bottom of the stomach, near the liver. Stop while still a little hungry, because eating to the full weighs on the soul. Do not drink straight after food, which chills the stomach and brings indigestion, 'deferred poison'; if he must, a little very cold water, and at the end a little wine mixed with about ten istars of water.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- An istar is a small weight; Steele's footnote (left out here) gives its value in drachms.",
  "arab.secretum.2.7":
    "After eating, a man should walk a little on soft carpets and then sleep on his left side, which is cold and needs warming. If his lower ribs feel heavy, he should wrap his stomach in a warm cloth or hold a warm-bodied girl. Sour belching is a sign of a cold stomach: drink hot water with oxymel and vomit, since spoiled food should not be kept.\n\nExercise before food lights the stomach's fire; after food it is harmful, because it pushes food on before it is digested. Sleep before food wastes the body; after food it nourishes, because in sleep the body's heat gathers round the stomach and helps digestion. That is why the wise prefer the evening meal to the midday one, which has to compete with the heat of the day and with the senses and the mind being busy.\n\nDo not eat again until the last meal is fully digested, which one knows by real appetite and saliva in the mouth. Food taken without hunger meets the body's heat asleep, like fire under ashes; food taken in true hunger meets it blazing. But do not put off eating when hungry either, or the stomach feeds on the body's waste and sends foul vapours to the brain. Changing from two meals a day to one, or the other way, or changing meal times, does harm. Habit is second nature, and a habit that must be changed should be changed a little at a time.",
  "arab.secretum.2.8":
    "Next the four seasons. Spring begins when the sun enters the first degree of Aries and lasts, by the authorities, 93 days, 23 hours and a quarter, from 21 Azar (March) to 23 Haziran (June). Day and night are equal everywhere; the air turns mild, snow melts, rivers fill and springs flow, plants leaf and blossom, animals breed, and the world is like a young girl in her finery. Spring is warm and moist like air and stirs the blood. Eat moderately: chicken, partridge, pheasant, lightly cooked eggs, lettuce, endive, goat's milk. It is the best time for bleeding and cupping, and one may indulge more in sex, purges, baths and sweating; the season itself corrects any mistake in treatment.\n\nSummer begins when the sun enters Cancer and lasts 92 days and 23⅓ hours, from 23 June to 24 September (Ailul). The air is hot, water dwindles, plants dry, and the earth is like a bride loaded with riches and lovers. Summer is hot and dry and spleen (black bile) dominates. Avoid hot foods, drinks and medicines and do not overeat; eat cooling foods (veal in vinegar, gourds, fat chicken, fine flour, sour apples, plums, sour pomegranates), use cooling scents and oils, drink iced water. Be very moderate in sex; avoid bleeding and cupping; baths are allowed. Vomiting is good in summer; purges and gargling only if needed.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The fractions of hours were lost in the scanned text and are restored from the page images.",
  "arab.secretum.2.9":
    "Autumn begins when the sun enters Libra and lasts 88 days and 17 1/12 hours, from 24 September (Ailul) to 22 December (Kanun I). Day and night are equal again, and then the nights grow longer. The air cools, north winds blow, rivers and springs shrink, plants dry, crops are gathered, animals hide or leave, and the earth is like a mature woman past her youth. Autumn is cold and dry: eat warm, soft, moist foods (chicken, mutton, sweet grapes, well-aged wine) and avoid what makes black bile. Exercise, sex and gargling are freer than in summer but less than in winter and spring. Bathe often; vomit, if needed, at midday or in the evening. For purging use agaric and dodder of thyme and whatever drives out bile.\n\nWinter begins when the sun enters Capricorn and lasts 89 days and 14 1/12 hours, from 22 December to 21 March (Azar). Nights are longest, then the days lengthen. It is bitterly cold and stormy, leaves fall, most plants die, animals shelter underground and in caves, the sky darkens, bodies weaken, and the world is like an old woman near death. Winter is cold and moist and phlegm dominates: seek warm things (young pigeons, young goat, roast meat, hot spices, figs, walnuts, garlic, thick red wine, warming electuaries), warm enemas, hot drinks, warm oils, a moderate bath. Avoid purging and bleeding unless necessary. Excess does little harm now, because the body's heat draws inward and digestion is strongest.",
  "arab.secretum.2.10":
    "The body has four parts, each with its signs of excess humours and its cure. The head: dim eyes, heavy eyelids, throbbing temples, ringing ears, a blocked nose. Boil wormwood in sweet wine with marjoram roots and rinse the mouth with it each morning; eat prepared mustard with food, and at bedtime a laxative powder of twelve herbs. The chest: heavy tongue, sour mouth and stomach, aching temples, cough. Eat less and vomit, then take bitter rose-water with aloes and mastic, and after food a walnut-sized piece of aniseed electuary. The stomach: wind, aching knees, shivering, fever. Take a mild laxative and follow the chest treatment. The bladder: loss of appetite and pimples on the genitals. Soak celery and fennel with their roots in white wine and drink it each morning with water and honey before breakfast.\n\nAn ancient book tells how a king asked the doctors of Rum, India and Persia for one medicine good for everything: the Rumi said hot water every morning, the Persian cress, the Indian myrobalan. Aristotle adds: go to bed on an empty stomach and fear no apoplexy; eat seven mithkals of currants each morning against phlegm and for memory; asafoetida in winter against fever; two walnuts, figs and rue against poison for the day. Guard the body's natural heat, which lives on its moisture; age comes from natural drying or from accident and disease.",
  "arab.secretum.2.11":
    "Diet. Food is light, heavy or moderate. Light food (flour, fat chicken, eggs) makes pure blood; heavy food suits hot temperaments and people who do hard work; moderate food (fine wheat bread, kid, yearling lamb, meats that are hot and moist) causes no blockages. Roast meat takes heat and dryness from the fire unless vinegar or lemon temper it. As a rule, set hot against cold in seasoning, unless hot food is wanted for a cold temperament or to rouse desire. Beef, camel and goat, being coarse and dry, soon turn to black bile; the meat of young castrated animals raised on green, shady pasture is best. Small, thin-skinned fish from sweet running water are better than big fat fish from the sea or lakes, which may be poisonous. Aristotle refers to another book of his on diet.\n\nWaters. Waters differ by region and soil. The best is light, clear, pure and sweet-smelling spring water far from buildings, quick to heat and cool, flowing from west to east. Avoid water with slime or worms, salt or muddy water, melted snow and ice, and stagnant marsh water in the sun, which breeds bile and swells spleen and liver. Cold water before food wastes the body; after food it heats and breeds phlegm. Drink cold water in summer and hot in winter.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The last sentences give two readings, marked (B) and (A) by Steele: one says cold water in winter, the other tepid water in winter, damages chest and liver and can kill suddenly.",
  "arab.secretum.2.12":
    "Wine from mountain grapes is drier than wine from well-watered plains: the first suits the old and the phlegmatic and harms the young, the hot-tempered and the thin; the second suits the young and hot-tempered. Old wine grows hotter and clears cold humours; bitter, sour wine is more medicine than food. Drinking every day harms everyone, and sweet wine spoils the stomach.\n\nThe best wine comes from land between mountain and plain, from sweet ripe grapes not pressed so hard that skins, pips and stalks give up their juice: golden, between red and yellow, with its dregs settled and clear. Taken in moderation, suited to season and constitution, it cleans the stomach, aids digestion, turns food into good blood, sends mild vapours to the brain, cheers the heart, loosens the tongue and gives the timid courage.\n\nExcess, enough to cloud reason and the senses, destroys brain, memory and senses, kills appetite, causes trembling, paralysis and melancholy, and ruins the liver. Wine is a poison like rhubarb, deadly to one who does not know the dose; yet as snake venom is the best cure for snakebite, wine cures many pains. Homer is said to have wondered how anyone who lives moderately on wine, bread and mutton could fall ill or die. After a bout of drinking, bathe in warm water and sit by a stream under an awning of willow and myrtle, sprinkled with rose-water. To give up wine, do it gradually, diluting it more and more until only water is left.",
  "arab.secretum.2.13":
    "Some things strengthen the body and some weaken it; some fatten, some thin; some moisten, some dry; some cheer, some tire. Strengthening: good wholesome food and light, pleasant things, taken in season and when needed.\n\nFattening and moistening: ease and comfort, sweet perfumes, a rich dish called asfedbajat, warm sweet fresh foods, sweet syrups and wines, fresh honey with walnuts in cool weather, all without excess; sleeping after food on soft beds, cool in summer and warm in winter; warm, sweet baths, not too long; seasonal flowers to smell (jasmine in winter, roses and violets in summer); an emetic three times a month, especially in summer, to clear the stomach. Joy, honour, victory, beautiful faces, good books, songs, friends' stories and fine clothes all help.\n\nWasting and drying: the opposite: too little food and drink, overwork, going about in the sun, long sleeplessness, sleeping hungry on hard beds, sulphurous, salt or (in winter) cold baths, sharp and fried food in summer, old unmixed wine, too much purging, bleeding and sex, and anxiety, poverty and fear.\n\nA bracketed note repeats it: moderate sex, wheat bread, fat chicken, vomiting with sweetened vinegar on summer mornings, and riding easy-paced animals add flesh; worry, grief, sleeplessness, an overbusy mind, too much love, sleeping on the ground or with old women and unpleasant sights wear the body down, and worst of all are evil thoughts and pursuing anxieties.",
  "arab.secretum.2.14":
    "The bath is one of the wise men's most wonderful inventions: it comforts and cleans the body, relaxes the limbs, opens the pores, drives out vapours and waste and cleans the skin after illness. Its four rooms are like the seasons, from cold winter to hot summer. The bather should pause in each room going in and coming out, never jumping from great heat to great cold or the reverse. It should be airy, with fresh water.\n\nIncense suited to the season should burn in it: in spring and summer ambergris of three or four mixtures, in autumn and winter of two mixtures with fresh aloes-wood. The bather sits on a soft cushion until he sweats freely, wipes himself with clean linen, then goes into the bathing place. When the heat grows strong he uses a cleansing soap suited to the season (sandalwood and myrobalan in spring and summer, myrrh and beet juice in autumn and winter), pours tepid water over his head, plunges until all dirt is gone, oils himself, comes out slowly, and is dried with towels scented with rose-water and ambergris, linen in summer, cotton and silk in winter.\n\nIf thirsty he drinks about half a ratl of rose and apple wine with cold water, rests looking at a beautiful picture or, better, a beautiful person, puts on scent, eats, drinks his usual mixed wine without getting drunk, and sleeps, avoiding sex that day and night. An old man should stay a shorter time, a young hot-tempered man only until he is wet through, a middle-aged man in between.",
  "arab.secretum.2.15":
    "This knowledge will free Alexander from all doctors. The pulse is a strong sign of health, but can only be learned by practice; Aristotle has written about it, and about urine, compound medicines, drinks and ointments, in other books. But since he has promised to reveal every secret, he will not hide the medicine called the 'guard', the 'hidden treasure of the sages'. Nobody knows who found it: some say it was revealed to Adam; some that eight great sages composed it in eight parts; some that Akhnukh (Enoch), Hermes the Great, whom the Persians call Ahjad, had it by revelation.\n\nFirst the medicated honey: 25 ratls of sweet pomegranate juice, 10 of sour pomegranate, 10 of sour apple, a measure of grape syrup and 10 ratls of sugar candy, simmered in a stone pot on a smokeless fire and skimmed until it is like honey.\n\nThe first medicine: roses, violets, parsley water, marjoram and ox-tongue soaked with myrobalan and cloves, boiled down, thickened with the honey and scented with musk and ambergris; it strengthens stomach, heart and brain. The second: myrobalan, cassia, liquorice and myrtle seeds boiled with honey and mastic; it cleans and strengthens the stomach, nerves, chest and brain. The third: myrobalans, cinnamon, galangal and nutmeg cooked with honey; it strengthens the inner and vital organs.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'Bahn-gentle' is the translator's alternative for the parsley water; the fractions are restored from the page images.",
  "arab.secretum.2.16":
    "The fourth medicine: bramble and celery juice, hyssop and sebestens boiled with the honey; it clears blockages and helps the chest and lungs. The fifth: lavender, barberries and aniseed boiled with honey; it gently drives out black bile and phlegm, cures melancholy, settles the stomach and breaks up wind. The sixth: fleawort, quince seed and gums in rose-water, boiled with honey; it soothes the chest, cures asthma, helps breathing and cools fever. The seventh: spikenard, cinnamon, cubebs and rhubarb boiled with honey; it heats the stomach and drives out wind. The eighth: Chinese rhubarb, doronicum, lac (and, in manuscript C only, three kinds of sandalwood), soaked and boiled with honey; it restores liver and heart.\n\nThe eight are then mixed with a quarter of their weight of stoned tamarind and boiled in a stone pot with 6 ratls of rose-water; when cool, balsam oil, ambergris and musk are added, then powdered pearls, rubies, emerald and gold. The mixture is kept in a porcelain or, better, gold pot scented with aloes and set under the open sky for a week, except on nights when the moon is unlucky or 'under the rays' of the sun. The result is one of the greatest treasures of kings: one mithkal after food each day and two on an empty stomach. It clears phlegm and bile, aids digestion, clears skin and sight, strengthens teeth, stops grey hair, sweetens breath, cures cough and palpitations, cheers the heart and sharpens the mind.",
  "arab.secretum.2.17":
    "Never take medicine or open a vein except at a time chosen by astrology; it greatly increases the benefit. Do not bleed or cup until the moon is full and 13 degrees from the sun, and avoid a long list of unlucky positions of the moon. The best time for bleeding is the second half of the lunar month, as the moon wanes, in Libra or Scorpio, free of unlucky stars; the worst is an unlucky moon in its second or eighth house. Cupping is best when the moon is rising and waxing, free of unlucky stars (above all Mars), in the house of Venus or watched by Venus or Jupiter, but never on the part of the body the moon rules at the time.\n\nFor taking medicine, the moon should be in a southern sign other than Capricorn and near Venus or Jupiter, or better in Scorpio, Pisces, Libra or the northern signs. Avoid the moon with Saturn, which keeps the medicine in the stomach; the moon with Mars does no harm but weakens it. Everything turns on the moon being well placed, away from the unlucky stars and near the lucky ones.\n\nThe soul's illnesses can be treated too, by music. Instruments carry to the soul, through hearing, the harmonies made by the motions of the heavenly spheres. Put into human sound, they make music that pleases the soul, because man's own elements are in harmony like the spheres; the nearer earthly music comes to the music of the spheres, the more the soul is stirred, gladdened and strengthened.",
  "arab.secretum.2.18":
    "When the soul is free of lust and pain it can find hidden truths from outward signs; this is the power of thought. When the soul rules the body and nothing comes between the spiritual substance in the heart and the animal part in the brain, the mind is cleared and things are reflected in it as in a mirror. That is the source of divination, of which books record many wonders, though it also depends on the stars at the time this power was formed.\n\nSo physiognomy, the reading of character from the body, is as necessary to Alexander as the other sciences of conjecture. One master of it was Aklimun, who read men's characters from the build of their bodies.\n\nHippocrates' pupils drew his portrait on parchment and asked Aklimun to describe the man. He studied the parts of the body and said: this man is deceitful, cunning and sensual, and loves fornication. The pupils wanted to kill him and called him an ignorant fool, for this was the great Hippocrates; he said they had asked for his reading and he had given it by his art. When they told Hippocrates, he said: Aklimun is right, and has not said one false word; this is my nature, but when I saw these qualities were bad I held myself back from them, and my reason overcame my passions. A philosopher who cannot master his desires by reason is no philosopher. This only added to Hippocrates' greatness, for philosophy is the mastering of desire.",
  "arab.secretum.2.19":
    "A short chapter on physiognomy, enough for one of Alexander's sense. The womb is to the child what the pot is to food, so a face very pale, bluish or very red shows imperfect 'cooking'; with any other defect it shows an imperfect body. Beware of such people, blue or very red and smooth: they are shameless, treacherous and sensual. Someone who often looks at Alexander and blushes, looks ashamed or has tears in his eyes when looked at loves and fears him; the opposite signs show an enemy. Beware of anyone misshapen.\n\nThe best-made man is of medium height, with black hair and slightly deep-set black eyes, a round face, white mixed with red or light brown skin, a well-proportioned body and head, little talk except when needed, a voice neither loud nor low. Choose him as a companion.\n\nThe parts: soft hair means timidity and little understanding, coarse hair courage; much hair on shoulders and neck, stupidity; on chest and belly, wildness and tyranny; red hair, stupidity and love of power; black hair, a mild nature and love of justice. Large bulging eyes, especially blue ones, mean envy, shamelessness and laziness; moderate, deep, dark eyes intelligence; slanting eyes wickedness; turquoise-blue eyes with spots the worst of men. Noses, foreheads, mouths and lips are read in the same way: a moderate nose and a moderate, lined forehead are best; a wide mouth means courage, thick lips and long teeth stupidity, a thin face care and intelligence.",
  "arab.secretum.2.20":
    "A small sallow face means a vile, deceitful, arrogant man; a long face shamelessness. The best face is fairly broad and modest, with soft cheeks, thin lips, good teeth and not too much hair. Bulging temples and full neck veins mean a quick temper; very large ears, foolishness with a good memory; very small ears, a stupid, thieving, sensual coward. A strong voice means courage; a moderate voice and speech neither fast nor slow mean wisdom and truthfulness; fast speech with a soft voice shamelessness and lying; a strong nasal twang envy and deceit. Waving the body or hands while talking shows a boastful, deceitful chatterer; gravity and silence a sound nature.\n\nNecks, bellies, backs, shoulders, arms, hands, feet and gait are read likewise: a moderate neck is best; a big belly means stupidity and lust; long arms reaching the knees courage and generosity, short arms cowardice and mischief; long palms and fingers skill and good government, short thick fingers stupidity; moderate, well-shaped feet are best; long slow steps mean success and prudence, short quick ones haste and ill will.\n\nThe best man has a moderate mouth, soft flesh, moderate height and build, fair skin tinged with red or clear brown, an oval, even face, hair between red and black, moderate deep-set eyes, a clear voice, and is grave, thoughtful, friendly, cheerful and high-minded. Choose such a man for company and government. But never judge by one sign alone: weigh them all and follow the stronger.",
  "arab.secretum.3.1":
    "Discourse III, on the form of justice. Justice is one of God's noble attributes. The king whom God has set over His people, with power over their lives and goods, is like a god and should resemble Him in mercy and wisdom. Right is opposed to oppression, and the opposite of oppression is justice. By justice the heavens stand over the earth; for it the prophets were sent; it is the form of reason God gives to those He loves best. By it lands are peopled, kingdoms stand and rulers are kept safe.\n\nThe Indians say a just king is better for the people than plenty, and better than steady rain. All things are made from elements; the cause is the action that made them, the maker is God, and the element's receiving the action, as far as it can, is justice.\n\nSo justice is of two kinds. Open justice is visible in the Creator's works as equality of weight and measure (the word 'equity' means equality). Hidden justice is belief in the Creator and His creation and the search for His word. Since the king resembles God in power, he should resemble Him in action, give his people laws and customs that agree with justice and divine command, and believe in them himself, because law draws its strength from belief: when people see the king following his law, they follow it and are at peace.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The translator marks 'principle' as uncertain, offering 'element?' as the alternative.",
  "arab.secretum.3.2":
    "Aristotle has made a diagram, based on philosophy, law and religion, that sums up the whole government of the world, every rank of people and the justice due to each. It is divided like the heavenly spheres: start anywhere and it leads round to the next, as the heavens turn. He begins it with the world. Had he sent nothing else, it would have been enough.\n\nThe circle: the world is a garden fenced by sovereignty; sovereignty is lordship upheld by law; law is guidance that governs the king; the king is a shepherd who musters the army; the army are dragons fed by money; money is food gathered by the people; the people are servants ruled by justice; justice is happiness and the foundation of the world, and so back to the world.\n\nIt is then restated several ways. In negatives: no justice without subjects, no subjects without a king, no king without an army, no army without a state, no state without soldiers, no soldiers without money, no money without population and peace, and no population and peace without justice and government. A saying of 'the Imam' adds: no state without men, no men without wealth, no wealth without subjects, no subjects without justice, no justice without law, which is the life of the state.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The diagram itself is not printed; the English gives its sentences in a line, and the same circle is set out in several versions.",
  "arab.secretum.4.1":
    "Aristotle opens the fourth discourse by saying he has gathered here the sciences of philosophy and some divine secrets about reason. The first thing God made was a simple spiritual essence holding the forms of all things, called Reason. From it came the Universal Soul, and from that Matter (hyle). Matter given length, breadth and depth became body, and body took the best shape, the sphere.\n\nFrom this came the heavens and stars, finest first and coarsest last: nine spheres one inside another, from the all-enclosing sphere through the sphere of fixed stars, Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, the Sun, Venus, Mercury and the Moon, and then the sphere of the elements, fire, air, water and earth, with earth at the centre as the coarsest body. As the spheres turned, the elements mixed, and over long ages minerals, plants and animals formed. Minerals grow inside the earth from trapped vapours and are mostly earth; animals, which move and feel, are mostly air. Plants rank above minerals and animals above plants.",
  "arab.secretum.4.2":
    "Man is the noblest animal and is mostly fire. He joins every kind of being, a coarse body and a pure heavenly soul, so whoever wants to know all things should begin by knowing his own soul.\n\nThe universal soul flows from reason and runs through all bodies like sunlight through air. It has a perceiving power and an active power, and seven faculties: attracting, holding, digesting, expelling, nourishing, forming and producing. These shape the body in the womb over nine months. Then come stages: speech by about four years, reasoning at fifteen, wisdom at thirty, a spiritual power that leads towards God at forty, and a religious power that prepares for the next world at fifty. A soul made perfect before it leaves the body is carried up to the heavenly assembly and at last joined to Reason.\n\nGod made man's body like a city with reason as its king, and gave that king five ministers, each with its own uniform and its own skill, to bring him what helps and warn him of what harms. They are the five senses: eyes, ears, tongue, nose and hands.",
  "arab.secretum.4.3":
    "The five senses are described. Sight has ten objects: light, darkness, colour, body, shape, place, distance, nearness, movement and rest. Hearing takes in two kinds of sound. Living sounds are either meaningful, like speech, or not, like neighing and birdsong. Lifeless sounds come from things striking, from thunder, drums and lutes. Every sound has a tune and its soul is its music, carried by the air to the ear and on to the imagination. Taste, in the tongue, knows nine flavours: sweet, bitter, salt, greasy, sour, sharp, astringent, insipid and styptic. Smell knows pleasant and unpleasant. Touch, in the hand, knows hot and cold, rough and smooth, and lies between two layers of skin.\n\nEach sense hands what it gathers to fine nerves at the front of the brain, servants of the king, reason. The imagination receives them and passes them to the thinking faculty in the middle of the brain, which judges what is harmful or useful. Perfection rests on fives: five planets, five kinds of animal (man, birds, small beasts, large beasts, reptiles), five parts of a plant, five notes in music, and five extra days at the end of the year.",
  "arab.secretum.4.4":
    "So Alexander should copy God's design and have five ministers. He should consult each separately, hide his own view, never show whose advice he prefers or that he needs it, gather their advice in his mind as the brain gathers the senses, ask God's help, and lean towards the advice that goes against his own wishes. Hermes, asked whether the person consulted judges better than the one asking, said yes, because his judgement is not swayed by his own desire. If the ministers answer quickly and all agree, Alexander should argue against them so that they think again. He should keep secret which advice he will follow, never favour one minister over another (rivalry between ministers has wrecked many states), and not prefer a younger man unless his advice is better. Good counsel weakens as the body ages.\n\nJudgement also depends on the stars at birth. Astrologers staying with a weaver read the horoscope of his newborn son and foresaw a learned man who would manage kings' affairs. The boy refused his father's craft despite beatings, studied, and became a minister. In the opposite case, an Indian king's son was born to be a smith, and nothing could turn him from it; the fine-tempered swords of India are said to be his.",
  "arab.secretum.4.5":
    "Alexander should not look down on humble men. A man eager to learn, steady, patient and free of vices, especially one who is also high-minded, well born, eloquent and read in history, deserves friendship, and if he also has good judgement, discretion and firm faith in the king's law, no one is better fitted to be minister.\n\nNothing should be done without advice. In a Persian book a minister tells his king to consult his ministers one by one, in secret, on the gravest matters, because a secret told to one man is better kept; on ordinary matters he should consult them together. A wise king's advisers add to him as streams add to a river, and good counsel wins what armies cannot. Another Persian king told his son to take advice always, since he was only one man.\n\nAristotle's strongest advice is never to rely on a single minister. If five good ones cannot be found, there should be at least three: three is the least support, five the middle and seven the perfect number, as there are seven heavens, earths, planets and days of the week. One manuscript adds the seven phases of the moon. To test a minister, the king can pretend to need money: one who says to spend the treasury is no treasure, one who says to squeeze the people is worst, and one who offers his own wealth is truly loyal. A minister greedy for gifts serves himself and can be bribed by an enemy, so ministers should be kept close and kept from dealing with other kings.",
  "arab.secretum.4.6":
    "The best minister has fifteen qualities. He is sound in body. He understands quickly. He has a good memory. He is sharp, so a small hint leads him to the right answer. He speaks clearly and briefly. He knows the sciences, especially arithmetic, which proves a sharp mind. He is truthful, honest, good-natured and cheerful. He is moderate in food, drink and sex and keeps away from games and pleasures. He is high-minded and generous. Money and possessions mean little to him; he wants only to increase his king's glory and make him loved. He loves justice, hates oppression and helps the oppressed. He is bold in carrying out what he has found to be right. He knows all the king's spending and understands complaints and their remedies before they reach him. He is neither too talkative nor too fond of jokes, nor too withdrawn to mix with people. Finally, he does not drink too much wine, keeps an open house for locals and travellers, listens to all the news, and is ready to settle people's complaints and to be patient with them.",
  "arab.secretum.4.7":
    "No creature is more powerful than man, and every animal's character is found in him: he is brave as a lion, timid as a hare, sly as a fox, proud as an elephant, vain as a peacock, mute as a fish, harmful as a rat, and so on through a long list. Nothing in the universe has a quality that man lacks.\n\nAlexander should never take advice from anyone without faith in God. The warning is a story told with a hostile picture of Jews that is the text's own, not a fact. A fire-worshipper (a Mage) riding a well-stocked mule meets a Jew on foot. The Jew says his faith allows him to harm anyone outside it; the Mage says his faith is to wish everyone well, even animals, and trust a just God. When the Jew points out that he is tired and hungry, the Mage feeds him and gives him the mule, and the Jew rides off and leaves him in the desert. The Mage prays that God will show His justice. Further on he finds that the mule has thrown the Jew and broken his neck and leg. The mule comes back to the Mage; the dying Jew begs for pity, and the Mage takes him to the city, where he dies a few days later. The king hears the story, befriends the Mage, and makes him his vizier. The moral drawn is that God repays each person according to his faith and deeds.",
  "arab.secretum.5":
    "The fifth discourse is about the scribes who write the king's orders. Choosing them shows how wise the king is. Meaning is the soul of speech, words are its body and handwriting its ornament, so a scribe must put perfect meaning into fine words, well written. Because the scribe shares the king's secrets, he must be trustworthy, honest, loyal and aware of the king's aims and what his affairs lead to; otherwise he will ruin him. The king should still watch his work so that nothing foreign creeps into his records, reward him for his loyalty and effort, and treat him as a free man whose fortunes are tied to the king's own.",
  "arab.secretum.6":
    "The sixth discourse is about ambassadors. A messenger shows how wise his sender is: he is the king's eye, ear and tongue where the king cannot be. So the king should pick men of perfect wisdom, judgement, dignity, appearance and honesty, explain what he wants, and then leave them to find the right course. If no such man can be found, he should send someone faithful who will neither add to nor cut the message and will remember exactly what he hears. At the very least the messenger must deliver the message to the right place and bring back the reply. A man who hopes to profit from the place he is sent to should not be sent, nor a wine-drinker: the Persians used to press visiting envoys to drink, knowing that one who drank would spill his secrets, and to offer them wealth, knowing that one who took it had sold his kingdom. The king should never send his vizier as ambassador, because letting him leave the court corrupts the state. Everything depends on trustworthiness; without it the envoy will betray the king for gifts and bribes.",
  "arab.secretum.7":
    "The seventh discourse is about officials who oversee the subjects and their taxes. The people are the king's treasury, one that never runs out and on which his government stands. He should treat them like an orchard of trees, not like a crop that is reaped once and must be sown again; two copies add that his own position depends on his exchequer. So he must care for the people, look after their interests and protect them from harm. Their overseer should know their affairs and be independent, honest, faithful, gentle and patient, someone who picks the fruit without harming the trees. The king should not appoint too many tax officials, because each will try to outdo the others by corruption, enrich himself at the people's expense and favour his helpers.",
  "arab.secretum.8":
    "The eighth discourse is about organising the army. Armies are the essence of the state, and the king must arrange his so that he knows the state of every part, near or far, can count them easily and can call up any number at once. There should be at least four chief officers, one for each side and each direction of the compass, or ten, since 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 makes ten, the perfect number. Each commander (amir) has ten officers (nakibs) under him, each nakib ten ka'ids, each ka'id ten 'arifs and each 'arif ten soldiers, a hundred thousand fighters in all. To send ten thousand, a hundred or ten men, the king simply calls up the right level. Since everyone looks after only ten, there is less confusion and the king's business stays orderly.\n\nThe army also needs a careful, learned and honest scribe to hand out pay without mistakes, since errors breed discontent. If he is found at fault, he should be dismissed publicly so that the soldiers know why. He should be good-natured and easy to approach and should have no other job. The king must also keep the means that kings need in peace and in war.",
  "arab.secretum.9.1":
    "The ninth discourse covers war: its tactics, how to avoid its harms, how to meet the enemy and when. Alexander should not fight in person and should keep his great officers near him. Whenever two kings meet, one plots against the other; such is human nature, as Cain's envy of Abel shows.\n\nWar has a body and a soul. Its body is the clash of two armies; its soul is each side's belief that it will win. When one side loses that belief, the war dies. So the king should stir his troops' confidence with reasons, talk with them as friends, and promise rewards that he actually pays on time.\n\nWhen defending against an invader, he should fortify well, post watchmen and scouts day and night, camp against a mountain with plenty of water and food, and use loud, frightening instruments to hearten his men and scare the enemy. His troops should wear varied armour, and detachments should take images, wooden towers holding archers, and fire machines. Swordsmen and wrestlers go on the right, spearmen on the left, and archers, fire-throwers and noise-makers in the centre on high ground overlooking the enemy. He should strike wherever the enemy line falls into disorder, stand firm (armies are mostly beaten by fear), hide men in ambush to make noise and seem more numerous, dig covered pits and slippery ditches, and keep many Khurasan pack animals, which frighten horses.",
  "arab.secretum.9.2":
    "In a siege the king should use catapults and slings and poisoned arrows, poison the enemy's water if he takes it, and dig under their walls. He must guard against night attacks, which rout armies and lose their goods. He should not chase a beaten enemy far, which the text calls treacherous and cruel. Cunning should come first and fighting last, to save honour and the army.\n\nThe text then gives sweeping stereotypes of peoples as soldiers: Indians as cunning and treacherous but not brave, Scythians as determined but weak, Dailamites as hard-hitting but faint-hearted, Turks as very brave and very foolish but good for war, so the king should keep many Turks close to him. Each people should be set against soldiers of their own kind. Small troubles should be settled before they grow, and everything should follow astrology.\n\nThe standards should be tied when Leo is rising, with the moon well placed and aspected by Mars. For a journey, the rising sign stands for the road, the seventh house for the destination, the tenth for its purpose and the fourth for its result. Alexander should not set out when the moon is unlucky, under the sun's rays, or in the sixth or twelfth house, or when Mercury is under the rays or going backwards. Jupiter well placed promises success. For war, the lord of the rising sign should be in a house of Mars, with Mars friendly, Jupiter in the fourth house and the moon good.",
  "arab.secretum.9.3":
    "Aristotle now reveals a secret he says he used in Alexander's service and kept from him: before fighting, find out the enemy commander's name and fight only if victory can be worked out. Each Arabic letter has a number, given in a table: A 1, B 2, J 3, D 4 and so on to 10, then tens to 90, hundreds to 900, and a sign for 1,000 (the English gives the letters in Latin script and leaves out the Arabic). Add up the letters of each commander's name, divide each total by 9 and keep the remainder, counting 0 as 9.\n\nNine tables then say which remainder wins. For example: 1 and 9, the 1 wins; 1 and 8, the 8 wins; 2 and 9, the 9 wins; 3 and 9, the 3 wins; 8 and 9, the 9 wins. When both remainders are the same, the attacker wins. Aristotle tells Alexander to study this science and use it wisely, and he will win, God willing.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The tables are not fully consistent as printed: 2 and 1 appears in the second table although 1 and 2 was already given (two beats one in both), and several pairs are given only once.",
  "arab.secretum.10.1":
    "The tenth discourse is on talismans, the secrets of astrology, winning hearts, and the powers of stones and plants. Aristotle reminds Alexander that the whole world, high and low, is one substance; only its outward forms differ, first as the four elements and then as minerals, plants and animals. Every physical body has a model in the spiritual world that causes and rules it. The seven planets reflect those heavenly forms in their light, as a mirror or an eye reflects things, and cast them down on the earth, stamping each thing as far as it can take the stamp, more or less fully depending on how long it is exposed. That lasting stamp is what talismans use.\n\nThe best talisman is Saturn's, the eldest planet and father of the rest; then Jupiter's, the sage of heaven; then the moon's, swift and full of wonders, made when her light is clear and her rays favourable for good work, or the reverse for harm. The wise who learned this science kept it to themselves so that it would not reach people unfit for it, but Alexander is worthy. Forms never grow or shrink; only matter comes and goes, and forms last because the planets keep reflecting them. So Alexander must know the motions of the heavens, the signs and planets, the dragon's head and tail, and the planets' meetings, aspects, risings and settings.",
  "arab.secretum.10.2":
    "He must learn to find an exact nativity, with or without an astrolabe, along with the angles of the chart, how long a planet stays in a sign, and when the lucky planets rise. Every body belongs to one planet in general and to several in its parts: man to the Sun, and his head also to the Sun; minerals to Saturn, and lead in particular to Saturn. To bind spiritual powers to a body, one finds the matching nativity and its ruling spirit, makes sure Saturn looks on it kindly and the moon is near the dragon's head and tail, and then fixes the heavenly form to the body.\n\nThen comes a talisman of sovereignty, which Aristotle says the greatest philosophers entrusted to him and he has told no one else. It makes people submit, frightens and sickens enemies, causes love and hatred, and wards off harm. Equal parts of the metals of Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, Venus, Mercury and the moon are taken, with as much again of Saturn's and a proportion of gold, and melted on a Thursday morning in the hour of Jupiter, when Leo is rising and the planets stand in set signs (the Sun in Aries, Saturn in Aquarius, Jupiter in Sagittarius and so on), best of all when the moon is joined with the Sun. The metal is made into a signet ring set with a square red ruby engraved with a winged, crowned black man riding a lion and holding a standard, with six winged, beardless men before him. Alexander is to wear it.",
  "arab.secretum.10.3":
    "Aristotle now reveals a closely guarded divine secret, sworn to silence among the sages so that it would not reach weavers, horse-doctors, smiths and carpenters who might ruin farming and childbirth. Take the egg made in the air and hatch it under a white bird. Kill the chick: red, white and yellow blood will flow, and the red is the one needed. The talisman itself is written in signs that the English does not print.\n\nOn the nights of Mercury, Jupiter, Venus and Saturn it is perfumed with incense suited to each planet while the operator walks round it, usually seven times, calling the planet's names, which are again given only as signs. On the night of Thursday it is shown to the stars, perfumed with all seven incenses and circled 49 times, and the names of the Sun are called. Then the kingdom of the world will belong to Alexander, and spirits will tell him in his sleep that the work has been accepted. To use it, he should fast for a day, break the fast with unusual food, sleep on his left side with the ring hung over his heart and ask the spirit to show him what he hopes for; someone will appear in a dream to guide him. A general who wears it and calls on Mars always wins, and anyone who holds it gains honour and power. Alexander needs no other charm.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'The egg that is created in the air' is printed without explanation and may be a coded name for an alchemical substance rather than a real egg.",
  "arab.secretum.10.4":
    "The wonders of talismans are many. Hermes the great invented one, called Zarzoori, made from olive down, and thanks to it Rome, which grows no olives, has more olives than anywhere else without planting a tree. A talisman made by Balbas or Arinush, king of Persia, calms winds strong enough to kill. Another puts out fire, which cannot be lit again until it is asked for help.\n\nAristotle reminds Alexander of the talismans he made for him: one that drove snakes, scorpions, leopards and every venomous creature from any land Alexander was in; the splitting of the earth that broke down the ramparts the Indians built against him; the mirror set up at Alexandria, one of the wonders of the world; and the mirror at the palace gate that shows hidden things and thefts and where lost property lies. There was also a hunting talisman carried on a spear that drew wild animals close without their seeing Alexander, and a brass figure on a rock by the shore at Alexandria that brought fish to be caught by hand. When a talisman guarding a treasure in the pyramid of Hayatil put Alexander's diggers to sleep, Aristotle made a counter-talisman and Alexander won the treasure.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The names 'Zarzoori', 'Balbas' and 'Arinush' are given as printed; the forms behind them in the Arabic are uncertain.",
  "arab.secretum.10.5":
    "Some historians say the treasure that the talisman guarded included a golden tomb 10 yards long and 2 yards high. Inside was a coffin holding a body perfectly preserved, hair and eyes included, wearing a crown cut from a single red ruby and weighing 10 ratls, and lying on a bed of large, precious pearls. On its chest lay an emerald tablet 3 yards by 1, inscribed in Syriac letters.\n\nThe inscription begins with the Muslim blessing 'In the name of God, the most compassionate, the most merciful' and says: everything that has a beginning has an end. The dead ruler claims to have ruled the four quarters of the inhabited world for a thousand years; his empire's income in a single day matched this tomb in weight and its contents in value; the Sun, Moon and wind obeyed him; he found the secret of nature and the furthest knowledge of creation; and he rose to the heavenly assembly in his pure spirit, leaving his earthly body to decay, so that those who come after should know that the true sovereign is the one who never dies.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Steele's footnote says this episode is found in only one of the Arabic manuscripts used, so it may be a later addition to the book.",
  "arab.secretum.10.6":
    "This section is headed as information on refining hard substances and on 'the spirits', which include the eagle, a bird of prey. Aristotle then gives a great secret. Take the stone that is animal, vegetable and mineral at once, the stone that is not a stone and has nothing of a stone's nature, though it looks like mountain stones and also like plants and animals. It is found everywhere, at all times and with every person; it has every colour and contains all the elements; it is the small world (microcosm). Its common name is the egg: the philosopher's egg.\n\nDivide it into four parts, one for each element, and recombine them in balance so that they join without fighting. In detail: the four can be separated only by a fire that does not destroy. Separate water from air, air from fire and fire from earth. Then work on the air and the earth with moisture and heat until they resemble each other and stop opposing each other, and add the two active forces, water and fire, to finish the work. Water drawn off alone will be white, and fire drawn off alone will be red.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The heading about 'the eagle' and 'the spirits' is hard to follow in the English; in alchemical language these probably name volatile substances, but the text does not say so.\n- Steele's footnote at the end of the Tablet says 'this section' is found in only one Arabic manuscript; it does not say whether that includes this recipe.",
  "arab.secretum.10.7":
    "This is the book's version of the Emerald Tablet, introduced as a saying of 'our ancestor Hermes Trismegistus'. The lower comes from the higher and the higher from the lower, and together they work wonders by a single operation, just as all things were made from one essence. Its father is the Sun and its mother the Moon; the air carried it in its womb, and the earth nursed it. It is the father of all talismans, the treasure of all wonders, perfect in power. If it becomes earth, the earth is separated from the fine, pure fire gently and wisely. It rises from earth to heaven and comes down to earth again, and so gains the powers of both above and below. Whoever has it has the light of all lights, and darkness flees from him. It is the power of powers, overcoming every fine thing and passing through every solid one, as the Mighty and All-Knowing arranged. Hermes ends: this is my glory, and this is why I am called Hermes Trismegistus, the 'threefold', because of the wisdom revealed to me.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The English is a literal translation of a short, difficult Arabic text, and several lines (for example 'If it becomes earth' and 'overcomes every ethereal object') could be read differently.\n- Steele presented this as the oldest known text of the Tablet; an Arabic version in the 'Secret of Creation' (Sirr al-khaliqa) is now generally thought to be older.",
  "arab.secretum.10.8":
    "Some wonderful properties of stones. One stone, which masters water and wind, is found dry and growing in the sea. If a piece weighing 8 carats is tied to the king's standard, every army that faces it will be routed.\n\nTwo more stones, one white and one red, are found in a freshwater river near the land of perpetual darkness. The white one rises to the surface at sunset, is fully visible at midnight and sinks out of sight by sunrise; the red one does the opposite, appearing at sunrise. A 2-carat piece of the red stone hung on a horse's forehead makes it neigh without stopping, while the white stone keeps a horse silent, which is useful in night attacks and ambushes. And if the white stone is placed on the mouth of someone in a lawsuit, he can speak only if he is in the right.\n\nThe section ends by promising more properties of stones, but the text moves straight on to plants.",
  "arab.secretum.10.9":
    "Plants come after minerals and take their shapes in the same way. Water dominates in plants as earth does in minerals, so plants form as water does when wind ruffles its surface: blow on a drop and it stretches and splits into three-, four- or five-sided shapes like leaves. Each planet makes what resembles it: Saturn earth, Jupiter water, Mars air, the Sun fire. Plants that do not flower belong to Saturn and those that do to Jupiter; the date palm, fruiting without flowers, belongs to Saturn and the Sun, and plants that flower without fruit to Jupiter and Mars. Every plant has a power matching a planet, and the universal soul orders them all.\n\nAlexander should learn which plants cause or cure sickness, bring joy or sorrow, love or hatred, true dreams, merriment, sloth, death or healing. Examples: a fragrant tree with cone-shaped leaves and fruit gives honour to whoever picks it in his own name, and helps insect bites; a striped herb does the same; a tree with branches lying on the ground makes the bearer merry and unbeatable; a red-flowered plant brings laughter and makes a woman touched with it incline to the man; seven seeds of a Chinese herb, Darufshoon, given in food under the right stars, make a person love the giver; a herb with square stems and blue flowers cures headache, colds, dizziness, sorrow, fear and epilepsy by its smell. Aristotle says he has fulfilled his promise, and the book closes with praise to God and blessings on the Prophet Muhammad and his family.",
};
